Z||000000||Formula||09-27-1999||06:12 PM||||My oil pressure drops almost into the red when I idle.  I know it burns oil cause of the oil smelling white smoke on start up. It also leaks a tad. Anybody else's oil drop down at idle.  One of my friends said that this keeps it from blowing the head gasket when you give a good cob-web blower (punching it).||||reg||1
Z||000001||WisconsinGT||09-27-1999||06:30 PM||||Formula...I have the same thing. I think it is normal on Fiero's.||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||09-27-1999||06:35 PM||||My 87 GT drops to around 38 lbs at idle, which seeems a little low also.  When i'm cruizing down the highway, it sits at around 70lbs.  Yours sounds like it might be getting dangerously low, but as long as it increases with the engine speed, you should be ok.  Just my opinion.||||reg||1
Z||000003||88 Fiero||09-27-1999||06:43 PM||||Pressure shouldn't drop that low...you probably want to check you oil pressure sensor...and probably check your oil pump...||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||09-27-1999||06:47 PM||||hook up an auxilary gauge and test..... If the pressure is consistantly dropping much below 40PSI you have a problem. If the electric gauge is just off you'll know real quick.||||reg||4
Z||000005||Mark||09-27-1999||07:33 PM||||Low oil pressure indication could also be an electrical problem.  Does your volt meter drop much below 13 volts during idle with lights or A/C on?  I had a loose ground connection (the copper braid from engine block to body) which was the cause of a low voltage condition as well as abnormally low oil pressure indication at idle.  Tightening one bolt fixed my indication problem.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Greg Piet||09-27-1999||07:52 PM||||I noticed that as well, but in the chilton manual it tells one to check oil pressure at 2500 rpm (around there), and it should be in a specific range(35-50???).<BR>So according to chilton my pressure is fine.  I have replaced the sensor, and in the morning on cold starts the pressure is high untill the oil warms up.  This makes me think my sender and gauge are okey dokey.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Eddie||09-27-1999||08:22 PM||||My car is a 2.5  it has less than 5000 miles<BR>on a blue printed motor and my oil gage when cold is at 48 when warm 42 at 2600 rpm its abuot 43, 42 AND IT ALSO IS STILL A PAIN IN THE ASS BUT I LOVE IT<BR>		Eddie ||||reg||1
Z||000008||terryk||09-27-1999||08:27 PM||||The spec for the V6 is 10PSI at idle and 50-55PSI at 2000RPM. For the L4, it's 36-41 at 2000 RPM. There is no spec at idle for the L4. 10 PSI at idle would be low side of normal, but ok. The Fuel Pump switch kicks out at 4 PSI. I know, pretty dang low!<P>Some V6's used an aluminum pump. While it is high volume and high pressure, it tends to fail. Later V6's used the cast iron pump. I don't know about the L4's. The gears would wear the housing down and lose pressure, especially at idle.<P>The gauges are balanced movements. Unless the battery voltage gets below about 10V, the gauges will read correctly (relative to what accuracy or lack of accuracy they normally have, although they are amazingly close except for the temp gauge.)<P>A bad pressure sender typically reads too high (pegged) or bounces all over the scale.<BR>If the gauge seems to move smoothly between high and low pressure, then the sender is probably ok, buy they do fail after about 3-4 years. Cheap to replace. <P>Both engines tend to show low oil pressure when the mains start to get loose. <P>The rule of thumb is 10PSI for each 1000 RPM, but keep in mind that the V6's had a problem with dumping #4 rod bearing. The HVHO pumps solved the problem but GM never figured out why.<P>A normal high RPM pressure for the V6 is 55+ and 70+ is not unusual. There is a blue stripe spring that is spec'd to provide 70+ above 2000 RPM at all times. The V6's can't get too much oil pressure although this is one reason they blow oil out every gasket on the engine (mainly the distributor O-ring).<P>Bad seals (smoke on start up) will consume oil, not it won't be reflected in the oil pressure.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||lowCG||09-28-1999||12:26 AM||||GM has been doing oiling systems this way(flow-oriented)since the fifties,no problem really.There's a balanced pressure throughout the motor,by design,so there's just no need to have any higher pressures. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||terryk||09-28-1999||02:20 AM||||My information is just from the designer of the motor and the X-11 engineer. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||DJRice||09-28-1999||08:43 AM||||GT, My 87 v6 does the exact same thing.  I havent been to concerned because it really doesnt ever drop much below 35-40 PSI.  At highway speeds it maintains about 70 PSI.  Last fall I replaced the Oil pressure Sender because it was leaking oil all over the place.  Before that, The gauge would read 70 no matter what RPM the engine was at.  <P>My brothers 86 v6 has a bit lower pressure, but his gauge jumps around alot.  We checked it with a different gauge and the pressure is fine, but that silly gauge jumps around like The House of Pain.||||reg||1
Z||000012||BlackGT||09-29-1999||12:12 PM||||TerryK<BR> You say that if the oil pressure guage is all over the place or it`s pegged you should<BR>change the sender....where is it and how do you do it?||||reg||1
Z||000013||Formula||09-29-1999||08:38 PM||||Mark,<BR>You hit the nail on the head I think. My voltage drops low when the <BR>ac is on.  Where is this ground at? ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Mark||09-29-1999||10:02 PM||||Formula,<P>Look for a copper braid between connected between the block and the passenger-side hood hinge.  Inspect the braid and tighten both connections.  To check it electrically, measure the voltage drop from the engine block to a good chassis ground.  It should be only a fraction of a volt.  I saw several volts drop on mine before tightening the bolts.||||reg||1
Z||000015||terryk||09-29-1999||10:26 PM||||On the V6, the sender is located on the pulley side of the engine on the right side of the water pump. It is positioned towards the top. It has a large connector with three wires. Since the A/C lines wouldn't clear the sender, they moved it from adjacent to the filter to this top location using a tube. <P>You will need to remove the battery and shield to get it out. Figure ~$45 for the sender.<P>Every pegged gauge has been the sender. Of course, the connector could be loose so check it first. I'm betting sender. They will read about right at idle or may jump. But when you rev it, it heads for 200 PSI on the gauge.||||reg||1
Z||000016||lowCG||09-30-1999||01:02 AM||||What's the range of the oil sender's resistance value?<P>I shook hands with Leonard Nimoy once,so don't even try to impress me with your engineer friends there Terry.<BR>The X-11 was a mars rocket wasn't it?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000017||DJRice||09-30-1999||08:29 AM||||The X-11 was also the shuttle in Armageddon, wasn't it?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||terryk||10-01-1999||01:36 AM||||"I shook hands with Leonard Nimoy once,so don't even try to impress me with your engineer friends there Terry.<BR>The X-11 was a mars rocket wasn't it?"<P>Be nice. My point was that I spent a fair amount of time researching the X-11 (the car). That's what they said. John Heinracy was a decent guy and told me the good and bad of the 660 engine. Do we hang? No.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||terryk||10-01-1999||01:37 AM||||LoadCG:<P>The sender range is 0-90 ohms. It's on the ground side of the meter bridge.||||reg||1
Z||000020||lowCG||10-02-1999||02:20 AM||||Whoa,sorrry,just trying to throw some humour into a potentially dry,technical issue.<BR>A buddy of mine was telling me that when he had looked at a new X-11,that it had tube headers,a quadra-jet,and made 176HP,is(was) this true?||||reg||1
Z||000021||terryk||10-02-1999||09:19 AM||||Hi LowCG,<P>Boy, I wish they had 176HP! All X-11's had cast iron exhaust manifolds although the 81's didn't have crossover pipes, instead they had Y pipe's. They used 2 barrel Vari-jet staged carbs and 140HP/150FT-LBS. In 85, they got MPFI with 135HP/160HP. The difference from the Fiero was strickly a rating issue.<P>Check out: http:\\www.foothill.net\~terryk\X11Z19.HTM<P>||||reg||1
